conomy as a  whole spends more on child care and  spends less on super yachts and  helicopters to the hamptons and  overall it's not inflationary at all  it sounds as if some of these senators i  know you teach economics for a living  could do with a lesson in macroeconomics  it's amazing how so many of our top  leaders elected politicians don't  understand  issues like the debt issues like the  deficit even the debate about  pay fors i feel like it's 2021 we've  been talking about pay falls since 2008  we've been talking about the need to pay  for things and deficits being bad i  thought we'd won this argument at least  amongst liberals or centrists or  democrats and yet again even after  donald trump came into office passed an  unpaid for tax cut we still have  democrats droning on about the need to  pay for things to pay for themselves  i i talk a lot i've even written a book  about zombie ideas things that just you  know should be dead but they just keep  on shambling along and eating people's  brains and um this yeah i mean fiscal  responsibility of course is only for  democrats and the the fact that there's  now a pretty wide consensus among  serious economists that the debt is not  a problem and nowhere close being a  problem at current levels has not i  think made its way through to the uh to  the policy-making elite um within the  buying administration they get it but  then i'm sure that a lot of senators  even on the democratic side are still  living uh you know a decade ago they  still haven't haven't caught on that  that we've learned something  yeah and let's talk about inflation uh  because even some uh very quote unquote  very serious people people you know well  like larry summers have kind of sounded  the alarm on that we did see new numbers  regarding inflation today consumer  prices in july were up 5.4 percent from  the year prior but so-called core  inflation is slowing down what does that  all mean for average americans who are  seeing their grocery bills or their  gas bills their cars that they're buying  get more expensive where do you predict  this will all go as the pandemic  continues and what does it mean for  democrats politically  well um a lot depends on how long this  goes on and uh um you know that that was  not  inflation is still running pretty hot  but uh you know some of us are talking  about team transitory those of us who  believe that we're looking at just kind  of a a bunch of temporary disruptions as  the economy tries to get itself back  together for after the pandemic  and the if you look at to the details  the numbers it looked a lot like you  know some of the things that have gone  way up in price are starting to level  off or even come down like used cars and  there's no sign that the inflation in  those very special  areas is spreading to the rest of the  economy so this is probably you know not  for sure  this is not uh exact science but but all  of this is probably a story for the next  few months and then we'll be able to  look back probably uh by the time the  midterm elections come around and say  what was all that fuss about this was we  did not fundamentally have an inflation  problem  what do you make just returning to joe  manchin and larry summers both of their  concerns that it's dangerous for  congress to spend as if we're in a great  recession or a great depression when  they say we're not so why spend like  that  well you need to really make a  distinction between the you know the the  american rescue plan the the big bill  that was passed in march which was  spending like we were in a depression  because we kind of were um and this next  initiative because that's already in the  past now and the stuff looking forward  is not we're not we're not we're not  talking now about massive spending  relative to this relative to the size of  the economy as i said you know 3.5  trillion sounds like a lot until you  realize that it's taking place since  it's over a decade in an economy with is  going to produce 300 trillion dollars  worth of stuff it just is not depression  level spending it's it's a a fairly  modest thing it's it's a boost for  around one percent of gdp in public  investment uh so now  i think there's a lot of smooshing  together you know of the issue was there  too much stimulus in march which is  still i think it was okay but that was a  legitimate question and then question of  but is this further spending a big risk  and it's not it's just if you do the  math it just doesn't work that way  so paul the next big economic fight  seems to be over raising the debt  ceiling senate minority leader mitch  mcconnell straight up told democrats to  include it in the budget reconciliation  because the gop won't back it later it  seems bizarre to me and to much of the  rest of the world watching that the  richest economy in the history of the  world goes through this ridiculous debt  ceiling brinksmanship every few years no  other western economy does this  nobody does this this is it's insane  congress passes bills dictating spending  and taxes but then it has to pass a  separate bill that allows the government  to borrow the amount of money that is  implied by the tax and spending  legislation that's already passed so  it's crazy uh and you know what we're  doing what we're seeing here is some  brinksmanship the biden people think  that in the end mcconnell will not force  the u.s economy into default  um and uh that's  you know i  i've learned not to take anything for  granted but it would be a heck of a  thing to do and and they they do feel i  think there's a widespread sense that  obama got rolled that obama allowed  himself to be bullied into into giving  into stuff because his opponents seemed  ready to bring the whole house crashing  down on top of everybody and biden  doesn't want to do that  you mentioned that they don't want to  get rolled like obama we hear that a lot  from people in the administration even  people like ron claim the chief of staff  who served in that administration about  lessons learned from the obama era you  were you know you chronicled the obama  years the debate over health care uh the  republican approach to everything that  happened on his watch the stimulus do  you think this administration joe biden  his former vice president and many of  the ex obama people in this  administration have learned the lessons  of that era sufficiently is that what  the first eight months has taught us or  not  i think in large part i mean there are  some people who worry that they wasted  too much time you know what there was  the political maneuvering involved in  getting a you know a partial  infrastructure bill done in a bipartisan  way was that worth it or should they  just have gone to reconciliation right  away  but that's political strategizing but  the fact the matter is that biden is  actually moving big and fast  you know the um uh if i i had  i was tearing my hair out during 2009  over over obama's caution over the all  of these half measures and the one thing  that biden is not doing is is proposing  half measures this this is big stuff he  is trying to get um a lot of important  stuff done while he has  well he has that the political power to  do it  paul you've been chronicling the right  descent into i don't know what to call  it insanity extremism for more than a  decade now in a recent column for the  new york times on the gop's climate and  coveted denialism you conclude and i  quote the rights dissent continues with  no bottom in sight  what does that mean for america what  does that mean for american democracy  which depends on a two-party system  yeah i don't know i mean i mean i'm i'm  fairly optimistic about you know current  policy developments i think we have good  people in charge who have a pretty good  idea of what they're doing but how long  can we function when  slightly less than half the country um  basically is is an allegiance to  complete craziness when  when people you know  it used to seem like a big thing  that republicans could believe that  climate science was all this vast  conspiracy but now they literally have  you know the hospitals in florida  are full to capacity children are dying  and they're still refusing to believe in  in the science refusing to believe from  what's right in front of their eyes how  long can  this democracy survive if that many  people are willing to  to go that far down  so on that one last quick question for  you as i say you've been covering this  stuff for a long time uh you were ahead  of the curve on many of these issues  especially the gop are you optimistic or  pessimistic about the future of american  democracy given all these different  headwinds  oh boy uh  this really depends on uh we go uh on  the on that how am i feeling after the  first cup of coffee i mean it's it's  it's scary as heck i mean if you if you  are terrified you're not paying  attention but but i'm not giving up hope  well said paul krugman thank you so much  for your time and your insights tonight  i appreciate it  hi i'm mehdi hussain thanks for checking  out our channel on youtube you can see  more of the mehdi hassan show by  clicking on any of the videos on this  screen and make sure you subscribe below  to stay up to date on the day's biggest  stories thank you for watching

